{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1778526759540,"sessionID":"ses_1e78c039dffevTsdiwmgAxHMzR","part":{"id":"prt_e1874aa71001aFojVAuHQl7qBU","messageID":"msg_e1873fd0e001grbCG1eFFIZHdg","sessionID":"ses_1e78c039dffevTsdiwmgAxHMzR","snapshot":"15f978b03c9d2fb7cd6167fcb0a542eb0a2b5898","type":"step-start"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1778526786947,"sessionID":"ses_1e78c039dffevTsdiwmgAxHMzR","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"chatcmpl-tool-b4a4a509d17af445","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"__issue_context__.json"},"output":"<path>/home/runner/work/eval-ont-agent-cl/eval-ont-agent-cl/__issue_context__.json</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: {\n2:   \"body\": \"The textual and logical definition for this term limits IELs to \\\"intestinal epithelium\\\" only, but IELs are found in multiple epithelial tissues - Gastrointestinal tract, Reproductive tract, oral cavity etc... \\n\\n1) Broaden the definition of \\\"intraepithelial lymphocyte\\\"\\n2) Add a specific subclass \\\"intestinal intraepithelial lymphocyte\\\"\\n\\n**Improved textual definition**\\nA tissue-resident lymphocyte located within the epithelial layer of mucosal tissues, particularly within the gastrointestinal, respiratory, and reproductive tracts. Characterised by permanent residency, this cell typically expresses CD103 (ntegrin alpha-E), which binds to E-cadherin on epithelial cells, enabling epithelial retention in both mice and humans. Phenotypically, IEL displays an activated, antigen-experienced state characterised by the constitutive expression of cytotoxic molecules (granzyme B, perforin) and innate-like receptors (such as NKG2D), enabling rapid, localised surveillance and immediate response to epithelial stress.\\n\\n**Add these references (DO NOT replace existing ones)**\\n\\nPMID: [29674648](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/29674648/)\\nWIKIPEDIA:Intraepithelial lymphocyte\\n\\n**Logical definition**\\n'mature T cell'\\n and ('located in' some 'epithelium')\\n and ('capable of' some 'mucosal immune response')\\n\\n--- \\n\\n**intestinal intraepithelial lymphocyte**\\n\\n**textual definition**\\nA T cell that is located in the intestinal epithelium and is capable of a mucosal immune response.\\n\\nPMID: [29674648](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/29674648/)\\nWIKIPEDIA:Intraepithelial lymphocyte\\n\\n**logical definition**\\n\\nEquivilantTo\\n'mature T cell'\\n and ('located in' some 'intestinal epithelium')\\n and ('capable of' some 'mucosal immune response')\\n\\n**ORCID**\\nhttps://orcid.org/0009-0000-8480-9277\\n\\n\",\n3:   \"comments\": [],\n4:   \"title\": \"Revise intraepithelial lymphocyte and subclasses\",\n5:   \"url\": \"https://github.com/obophenotype/cell-ontology/issues/3346\"\n6: }\n\n(End of file - total 6 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"{\n  \"body\": \"The textual and logical definition for this term limits IELs to \\\"intestinal epithelium\\\" only, but IELs are found in multiple epithelial tissues - Gastrointestinal tract, Reproductive tract, oral cavity etc... \\n\\n1) Broaden the definition of \\\"intraepithelial lymphocyte\\\"\\n2) Add a specific subclass \\\"intestinal intraepithelial lymphocyte\\\"\\n\\n**Improved textual definition**\\nA tissue-resident lymphocyte located within the epithelial layer of mucosal tissues, particularly within the gastrointestinal, respiratory, and reproductive tracts. Characterised by permanent residency, this cell typically expresses CD103 (ntegrin alpha-E), which binds to E-cadherin on epithelial cells, enabling epithelial retention in both mice and humans. Phenotypically, IEL displays an activated, antigen-experienced state characterised by the constitutive expression of cytotoxic molecules (granzyme B, perforin) and innate-like receptors (such as NKG2D), enabling rapid, localised surveillance and immediate response to epithelial stress.\\n\\n**Add these references (DO NOT replace existing ones)**\\n\\nPMID: [29674648](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/29674648/)\\nWIKIPEDIA:Intraepithelial lymphocyte\\n\\n**Logical definition**\\n'mature T cell'\\n and ('located in' some 'epithelium')\\n and ('capable of' some 'mucosal immune response')\\n\\n--- \\n\\n**intestinal intraepithelial lymphocyte**\\n\\n**textual definition**\\nA T cell that is located in the intestinal epithelium and is capable of a mucosal immune response.\\n\\nPMID: [29674648](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/29674648/)\\nWIKIPEDIA:Intraepithelial lymphocyte\\n\\n**logical definition**\\n\\nEquivilantTo\\n'mature T cell'\\n and ('located in' some 'intestinal epithelium')\\n and ('capable of' some 'mucosal immune response')\\n\\n**ORCID**\\nhttps://orcid.org/0009-0000-8480-9277\\n\\n\",\n  \"comments\": [],\n  \"title\": \"Revise intraepithelial lymphocyte and subclasses\",\n  \"url\": \"https://github.com/obophenotype/cell-ontology/issues/3346\"\n}","truncated":false,"loaded":[]},"title":"__issue_context__.json","time":{"start":1778526786935,"end":1778526786946}},"id":"prt_e18750eaf001b0SbR5rtlNtAj8","sessionID":"ses_1e78c039dffevTsdiwmgAxHMzR","messageID":"msg_e1873fd0e001grbCG1eFFIZHdg"}}
